WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.607 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
If a calculator shows an answer with one extra zero, how
could you spot it?

00:00:05.257 --> 00:00:08.698 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Today we are estimating addition and subtraction.

00:00:08.918 --> 00:00:15.610 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We will use nearby friendly numbers as benchmarks, then
decide whether an exact answer has a sensible size.

00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:20.724 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to use estimates to check whether an
answer is reasonable.

00:00:21.374 --> 00:00:23.125 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:23.345 --> 00:00:25.785 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can choose useful nearby numbers.

00:00:26.005 --> 00:00:29.339 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can keep an estimate separate from an exact answer.

00:00:29.559 --> 00:00:33.023 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can explain whether the answer has a sensible size.

00:00:33.673 --> 00:00:36.920 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An estimate is a useful approximate answer.

00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:40.214 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose a rounding place that suits the situation.

00:00:40.434 --> 00:00:47.486 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Keep the exact numbers for the final calculation; the
estimate helps you check the scale, not replace the exact
answer.

00:00:48.136 --> 00:00:53.691 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Estimate three thousand eight hundred and seventy-six plus
two thousand one hundred and forty-two.

00:00:53.911 --> 00:00:58.638 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Round to the nearest hundred: three thousand nine hundred
plus two thousand one hundred.

00:00:59.288 --> 00:01:03.790 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The exact total is six thousand and eighteen, close to six
thousand.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:10.085 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A result near sixty thousand would be unreasonable because
each starting number is only a few thousand.

00:01:10.735 --> 00:01:16.006 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Estimate eight thousand and thirty-two minus three thousand
nine hundred and eighty-seven.

00:01:16.226 --> 00:01:20.023 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Nearby thousands give eight thousand minus four thousand.

00:01:20.673 --> 00:01:25.332 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The exact difference is four thousand and forty-five, close
to our estimate.

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:30.790 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both rounding choices made the mental subtraction simple
enough to check the size quickly.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:36.453 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
An estimate is a helpful check, not a replacement for an
exact calculation.

00:01:36.673 --> 00:01:39.354 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
About six thousand tells us the scale.

00:01:39.574 --> 00:01:42.996 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Six thousand and eighteen is the exact total.

00:01:43.216 --> 00:01:46.895 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Both have useful jobs, but they answer different questions.

00:01:47.545 --> 00:01:53.628 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Would forty thousand be a reasonable estimate for four
thousand nine hundred plus one thousand nine hundred?

00:01:57.628 --> 00:01:58.665 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
No.

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:02.681 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five thousand plus two thousand is about seven thousand.

00:02:02.901 --> 00:02:07.980 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The exact answer is six thousand eight hundred, so forty
thousand is much too large.

00:02:08.630 --> 00:02:14.764 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Choose sensible nearby numbers, calculate the benchmark, and
compare its size with the exact result.

00:02:14.984 --> 00:02:17.475 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Different useful estimates are possible.

00:02:17.695 --> 00:02:20.117 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Always explain your rounding choice.

00:02:20.337 --> 00:02:23.496 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now choose a strategy and explain why it works.

00:02:23.716 --> 00:02:26.863 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Have a go at the lesson practice on MathMagic!
